// Practice Test 1 — Scenarios: Customer Support (1), Code Generation (2), Multi-Agent Research (3), Structured Extraction (6)
// ============================ QUESTION BANK FORMAT ============================
// To add a bank: copy this file to q4.js (then q5.js, ...) next to index.html. It is auto-discovered — no code changes.
// Keep file numbers consecutive (the loader stops after two missing numbers). Each bank needs a unique `id`.
//
//   { s: 1,            // scenario 1–6 (groups questions like the real exam; names live in js/tests.js)
//     d: 2,            // DOMAIN 1–5 — drives the "Domain accuracy" panel and drills:
//                      //   1 Agentic Architecture & Orchestration   2 Tool Design & MCP Integration
//                      //   3 Claude Code Configuration & Workflows  4 Prompt Engineering & Structured Output
//                      //   5 Context Management & Reliability
//     q: "Question text",
//     o: ["correct", "wrong", "wrong", "wrong"],   // correct option(s) FIRST; shuffled at runtime; keep all options the same length
//     a: [0],          // indexes (into o) of the correct option(s): [0] single answer, [0,1] = "select two"
//     e: "Explanation shown after answering." }
// ==============================================================================
// Authoring convention: the correct option(s) are listed first; the app shuffles options at runtime.
window.EXAMS = window.EXAMS || [];
window.EXAMS.push({
  id: 1,
  title: "Practice Test 1",
  questions: [
// ---------------- Scenario 1: Customer Support Resolution Agent ----------------
{s:1,d:1,q:"You are the backend engineer on a returns-support agent built with the Agent SDK. About 1 in 12 conversations dies mid-task: the agent says “Let me pull up that order for you,” and then the chat simply stops with no lookup. Your loop currently exits as soon as a response contains any text block. Which change fixes this without creating new failure modes?",
o:["Keep looping while stop_reason is “tool_use” and finish only on “end_turn”, whether or not the turn also contains text",
"Keep the text check but add a phrase matcher that ends the loop only on closers like “anything else I can help with?” as well",
"Replace the text check with a hard cap of ten iterations so that every conversation gets the same amount of tool work over time",
"Have the agent end every final message with a DONE token and stop the loop only after that exact token is detected as well"],
a:[0],e:"stop_reason is the authoritative loop signal: “tool_use” means run the tool and continue, “end_turn” means the model is finished. A response can contain text AND a tool_use block, so checking for text is an anti-pattern. Phrase matching and sentinel tokens parse natural language (unreliable), and an iteration cap as the primary stop is an explicitly named anti-pattern."},

{s:1,d:1,q:"After lookup_order runs for a customer asking about a delayed headset, your code sends the next request containing only a user message that holds the tool_result. The agent now replies as if it never heard the original complaint and asks the customer what they need. What is the correct way to continue the loop?",
o:["Append the assistant turn containing the tool_use block, then a user turn with the matching tool_result, to the full running history",
"Send only the newest tool_result in a fresh request so the agent concentrates on the most recent information it has received",
"Fold the order data into the system prompt and restart the conversation with just the customer's original opening message",
"Write the order data to an external cache and tell the agent to ask for it by key whenever it decides that it needs it"],
a:[0],e:"The Messages API is stateless. Every iteration must resend the full history: prior turns, the assistant message containing tool_use, and a user message with a tool_result (matching tool_use_id). Sending only the latest result drops all earlier context, which is exactly the amnesia described."},

{s:1,d:1,q:"Your company's refund policy says anything over $500 must be approved by a human. The system prompt states this plainly, yet a monthly audit of 4,000 refunds finds roughly 3% of refunds above $500 were processed by the agent alone. Finance wants this to be impossible, not unlikely. What do you implement?",
o:["A PreToolUse hook on process_refund that blocks amounts over $500 and redirects the case into the human escalation flow each time",
"A stronger system prompt that repeats the $500 limit in capital letters at both the beginning and the end of the instructions each time",
"A set of few-shot examples in the prompt that show refunds above $500 being escalated, so the pattern becomes more consistent each time",
"A temperature of zero on every refund conversation so that the agent's decisions always follow the written policy exactly"],
a:[0],e:"When a business rule requires guaranteed compliance, enforce it with a hook (PreToolUse on process_refund) for a deterministic guarantee. Prompt emphasis and few-shot examples are probabilistic and keep a non-zero failure rate, and temperature 0 does not make policy adherence guaranteed."},

{s:1,d:1,q:"Your order tools disagree: billing returns Unix epoch seconds, the order service returns ISO 8601 dates, and shipment status arrives as numeric codes (3 = shipped, 5 = delivered). Customers are being told delivery dates that are off by a day or by decades. You want the most reliable fix with the least ongoing maintenance. What do you do?",
o:["Add a PostToolUse hook that converts every timestamp and status code to one consistent format before the model sees the result",
"Add a section to the system prompt explaining each timestamp format and status code so the model can convert them correctly",
"Ask each backend team to standardize its API output and hold the agent rollout until all of the services agree on one format overall",
"Add a convert_format tool that the agent can call whenever it notices a value that looks like it might be in the wrong format over time"],
a:[0],e:"PostToolUse hooks intercept tool results and transform them before the model processes them, which is the pattern for normalizing heterogeneous data. Prompt guidance is probabilistic, re-engineering every backend is disproportionate, and a conversion tool depends on the model noticing the problem."},

{s:1,d:5,q:"A customer writes: “I just need to change my shipping address, but I want to talk to a real person, not a bot.” Changing an address is a task your agent handles routinely with its tools. You are writing the escalation rule for exactly this case. What should the agent do?",
o:["Escalate right away and pass a structured summary of the customer's details and request along with the escalation as well",
"Complete the quick address change first since it takes seconds, then offer to connect the customer to a human afterward",
"Explain that the change is simple and the agent can do it now, and escalate only if the customer asks a second time each time",
"Run a sentiment check on the message and escalate only when the frustration score is above the configured threshold each time"],
a:[0],e:"An explicit request for a human is an escalation trigger that should be honored immediately, without first attempting investigation or resolution. “Offer to resolve, escalate if they reiterate” applies to frustration WITHOUT an explicit request for a human. Sentiment scores are an unreliable proxy."},

{s:1,d:5,q:"A customer writes: “This is ridiculous, my package is late AGAIN!” They have not asked for a person. Your policy fully covers late-delivery compensation (a 15% credit) and the agent has the tool to apply it. Which response pattern should you build into the agent?",
o:["Acknowledge the frustration, offer the standard late-delivery credit, and escalate only if the customer then asks for a human",
"Escalate immediately, because strongly negative sentiment is a reliable sign that the case is too complex for the agent",
"Skip the emotional language entirely and reply only with the shipment status details returned by the order lookup tool over time",
"Ask the customer to rate their frustration from 1 to 10 and escalate automatically whenever the number is above 7 for the team"],
a:[0],e:"Frustration alone is not an escalation trigger when the issue is within the agent's capability. Acknowledge it, offer the resolution, and escalate only if the customer reiterates a preference for a human. Sentiment does not correlate with case complexity."},

{s:1,d:5,q:"A customer asks the agent to match a lower price they found at a competitor for a pair of headphones. Your policy documents describe only adjustments when your own site lowers a price within 14 days; they say nothing about competitors. How should the agent handle the request?",
o:["Escalate to a human, because the written policy covers only own-site price drops and is silent about competitor matching over time",
"Apply the own-site adjustment rule to the competitor price, since it is the nearest policy and it serves the customer well",
"Decline the request, because anything the written policy does not explicitly permit should be treated as prohibited",
"Approve the match whenever the price difference is under $20, since small amounts carry very little financial risk"],
a:[0],e:"When policy is ambiguous or silent on the specific request (a policy gap), the right move is escalation. The agent should not invent policy by analogy, refuse based on an assumed rule, or make up its own thresholds."},

{s:1,d:5,q:"A customer chatting about a refund gives only the name “John Smith.” get_customer returns three accounts, each with recent orders, and the refund would go to the original payment method. What should the agent do next?",
o:["Ask for an additional identifier such as the email, phone number, or order number before taking any account action each time",
"Pick the account with the most recent order activity, since it is the likeliest match for the person currently chatting",
"Use the first record returned, because the backend ranks results by relevance and the top match is usually the right one as well",
"Merge the three accounts' details into one view so the agent has the full picture before deciding what to do next"],
a:[0],e:"Multiple matches require clarification (ask for more identifiers) rather than heuristic selection. Picking by recency or rank risks acting on the wrong account, which is especially dangerous before a refund."},

{s:1,d:1,q:"Tier-2 human agents who take escalations from your support bot cannot see its conversation transcript, and they complain about re-asking customers everything. Average time to first action on escalated tickets is 9 minutes. Which change to the escalation payload helps most?",
o:["A structured handoff with customer ID, root cause, amounts already refunded, and a recommended next action for the human",
"The complete raw transcript, including every tool call and result, so that nothing at all is lost during the handoff overall",
"A short free-text note describing the customer's mood and the general topic of the support request they brought in",
"A reminder that human agents should re-verify everything with the customer, since the bot's investigation is not trusted"],
a:[0],e:"Structured handoff protocols (customer ID, root cause, amounts, recommended action) give human agents what they need when they lack transcript access. A raw dump is slow to act on, a mood note omits the facts, and re-verifying everything wastes the agent's work."},

{s:1,d:2,q:"Your process_refund tool returns the string “Operation failed” for everything: timeouts, invalid amounts, and policy violations alike. Logs show the agent retrying refunds that violate policy up to eight times per conversation. What is the best fix?",
o:["Return structured errors with an error category, an isRetryable flag, and a readable explanation the agent can act on",
"Add a system prompt rule limiting the agent to three retries on any failed tool call before it escalates the case",
"Wrap each tool in automatic retry logic with exponential backoff so the agent never sees a failed operation directly overall",
"Return a longer generic error message that includes the full stack trace from the backend refund service each time over time"],
a:[0],e:"Uniform error messages prevent appropriate recovery decisions. Structured metadata (errorCategory: transient/validation/business/permission, isRetryable, a human-readable description) tells the agent whether to retry, fix input, explain to the customer, or escalate. Blind retries waste attempts on non-retryable errors."},

{s:1,d:2,q:"A customer requests a refund for an order placed 112 days ago; your return window is 90 days, and the refund call fails on that rule. What should your MCP tool return so the agent handles the situation well?",
o:["isError true, a business error category, isRetryable false, and a customer-friendly note about the 90-day return policy",
"isError true, a transient error category, and isRetryable true so the agent attempts the refund a few more times as well",
"isError false with an empty result body, so the agent treats the refund as done and moves along with the conversation each time",
"isError true with only the raw HTTP 422 status code, leaving the agent to work out the cause from the code by itself overall"],
a:[0],e:"Business-rule violations are not retryable. Flagging isError, categorizing it as a business error, setting retryable false, and including a customer-friendly explanation lets the agent communicate the policy accurately instead of retrying or reporting false success."},

{s:1,d:5,q:"lookup_order returns an empty array both when an order does not exist and when the order database times out. During a 20-minute database slowdown, the agent told dozens of customers, “I can't find any order with that number.” What is the right fix?",
o:["Return timeouts as an isError transient failure, and reserve the empty result for successful queries that found no match",
"Instruct the agent to retry any empty result three times before it tells the customer an order cannot be found at all",
"Have the agent escalate every empty result to a human, since it cannot tell whether the lookup actually succeeded or not",
"Raise the database timeout to sixty seconds so lookups rarely time out and the empty results become trustworthy again"],
a:[0],e:"Access failures (timeouts) and valid empty results (a successful query with no matches) must be distinguishable. Returning a failure as an empty success silently suppresses the error. Fix the signal at the source rather than working around the ambiguity in the agent."},

{s:1,d:5,q:"In long support chats, older turns are summarized to save context. Customers now complain the agent “forgot” things like “you promised $47.50 back by Friday.” Which change most effectively prevents this?",
o:["Extract amounts, dates, order numbers, and statuses into a persistent case-facts block that is included verbatim in every prompt",
"Summarize the conversation more often so each summary is shorter and easier for the model to process accurately during normal use",
"Increase max_tokens on every request so that the model has more room to restate the important figures in its replies going forward",
"Tell the summarizer to be more careful with numbers and add a reminder that exact figures matter a lot to customers"],
a:[0],e:"Progressive summarization tends to compress exact numbers, dates, and customer-stated expectations into vague text. Keeping transactional facts in a persistent “case facts” block outside the summarized history preserves them reliably. max_tokens controls output length, not memory."},

{s:1,d:5,q:"Each lookup_order call returns 40+ fields (warehouse codes, internal audit flags, carrier metadata), but return decisions need about five of them. Long conversations keep hitting context limits. What should you do?",
o:["Trim each order lookup to the few return-relevant fields before the result is added to the conversation history",
"Move to a model with a larger context window so that the full 40-field payloads can accumulate without limits over time",
"Tell the model to ignore the irrelevant fields, since it can focus on what matters when it is clearly instructed",
"Call lookup_order less often by caching the first result and reusing it for every later question in the session each time"],
a:[0],e:"Tool results accumulate in context and consume tokens out of proportion to their relevance. Trim verbose outputs to the relevant fields before they accumulate (in the tool or a PostToolUse hook). Ignoring fields still costs tokens, caching risks stale data, and bigger windows only delay the problem."},

{s:1,d:1,q:"One customer message reports three problems: a duplicate charge, a damaged item, and a locked account. In testing, the agent fixes the duplicate charge and never mentions the other two. Which design is best?",
o:["Break the message into distinct issues, investigate each with shared customer context, and reply with one combined resolution",
"Handle only the first issue mentioned and ask the customer to open new conversations for each remaining problem from the start",
"Escalate any message that contains more than one issue, since multi-issue cases are too complex for an agent to own in practice",
"Address the issues in alphabetical order by category so the agent follows a consistent and predictable sequence every time each time"],
a:[0],e:"Multi-concern requests should be decomposed into distinct items, investigated (in parallel where possible) using shared context, then synthesized into a unified resolution. Forcing new conversations or escalating all multi-issue messages hurts first-contact resolution."},

// ---------------- Scenario 2: Code Generation with Claude Code ----------------
{s:2,d:3,q:"You are onboarding a new engineer. In their Claude Code sessions, the assistant ignores the team's naming conventions, but it follows them perfectly for the senior engineer who wrote them. The repository has no CLAUDE.md file. What is the most likely cause?",
o:["The conventions live in the senior engineer's personal ~/.claude/CLAUDE.md, so they should move into a project-level CLAUDE.md",
"The new engineer's Claude Code cache is stale, so clearing it and restarting the session will make the standards load",
"The conventions are too long to be loaded, so splitting them into several smaller files will let the model read them for the team",
"The new engineer must run /init in the repository so that Claude Code regenerates and loads the shared team standards over time"],
a:[0],e:"User-level ~/.claude/CLAUDE.md applies only to that user and is not shared through version control. Team standards belong in a project-level CLAUDE.md (root or .claude/CLAUDE.md) committed to the repo. /init would generate a new file from the codebase, not recover someone's personal instructions."},

{s:2,d:3,q:"In your repo, Claude Code follows the testing conventions in some sessions and ignores them in others. Before you change anything, you want to see exactly which memory files each session actually loaded. What should you use?",
o:["Run /memory to see which CLAUDE.md and memory files are loaded in the current session and open any of them to edit each time",
"Run /compact, which prints the list of configuration files that were loaded along with a summary of the conversation",
"Run claude --debug-config to dump every configuration source and the order in which they were merged at startup",
"Ask Claude to recite its instructions in the chat, since its answer reliably reflects each loaded memory file word for word"],
a:[0],e:"/memory shows which memory files are loaded and lets you open them, which is the documented way to diagnose inconsistent behavior across sessions. /compact summarizes the conversation, --debug-config does not exist, and self-reports are not reliable verification."},

{s:2,d:3,q:"Your monorepo's payments package must follow the security and API standards files, while the docs package needs only the writing style guide. You want each package to pull in just what applies, without duplicating text. How do you structure each package's CLAUDE.md?",
o:["Use @import in each package's CLAUDE.md to pull in only the standards files that apply to that particular package overall",
"Paste the full text of each standards file into every package's CLAUDE.md so that each package is self-contained as a result",
"Put every standard into the root CLAUDE.md and rely on Claude to work out which sections apply to the current package",
"Create one slash command per standard and ask developers to remember to run the right ones before they start coding"],
a:[0],e:"The @import syntax (for example @docs/security-standards.md) keeps CLAUDE.md modular and lets each package selectively include the relevant standards. Copying duplicates content and drifts, relying on inference is unreliable, and manual commands depend on memory."},

{s:2,d:3,q:"Your root CLAUDE.md has grown to 1,200 lines covering testing, API conventions, deployment, and security. Reviewing changes to it has become painful, and several teams edit the same file constantly. What restructuring is recommended?",
o:["Split it into focused topic files under .claude/rules/, such as testing.md, api-conventions.md, and deployment.md",
"Convert the whole file into a single skill so that it loads only when a developer invokes it with a command each time",
"Move the content into ~/.claude/CLAUDE.md on each machine so the repository no longer carries the large file",
"Delete any section older than six months, assuming that important conventions will be re-added by the team later"],
a:[0],e:".claude/rules/ is designed for organizing topic-specific rule files as an alternative to a monolithic CLAUDE.md. A single skill would stop universal standards from always applying, user-level files are not shared, and deleting by age loses valid conventions."},

{s:2,d:3,q:"Your infrastructure team wants Terraform conventions to load only when Claude edits files under terraform/, not in every session across the repo, to avoid wasting context. What is the correct configuration?",
o:["A .claude/rules/terraform.md file with YAML frontmatter paths: [“terraform/**/*”] so it loads only for matching files overall",
"A terraform/CLAUDE.md file with a frontmatter field when: editing that tells Claude to load it only during edit sessions",
"A .claude/commands/terraform.md file that developers invoke manually each time they begin working on infrastructure code",
"An entry in .mcp.json that maps the terraform directory to a conventions server which serves the rules on request in this case"],
a:[0],e:"Path-scoped rules live in .claude/rules/ with a YAML frontmatter paths field containing glob patterns, and they load only when working with matching files. There is no “when: editing” field, commands are manual, and MCP servers are not a rules mechanism."},

{s:2,d:3,q:"Your team built an /analyze-codebase skill. Each run dumps thousands of lines of intermediate output into the main conversation, and the developer's follow-up questions in the same session get noticeably worse. What change to the skill best addresses this?",
o:["Add context: fork to the SKILL.md frontmatter so the skill runs in an isolated subagent and returns only its final result",
"Add allowed-tools: Read to the frontmatter so the skill can only read files and therefore produces much less output as well",
"Move the skill into ~/.claude/skills/ so that its output is stored in the user's profile instead of the shared session overall",
"Add argument-hint to the frontmatter so developers pass narrower arguments that make the skill produce less output"],
a:[0],e:"context: fork runs the skill in an isolated subagent context, so verbose output does not pollute the main conversation. allowed-tools controls tool access (not output volume), location controls sharing scope, and argument-hint only prompts for parameters."},

{s:2,d:3,q:"Developers keep running your /migrate skill with no arguments, and Claude guesses which table to migrate, sometimes wrongly. The skill needs a table name every time. Which SKILL.md frontmatter option is designed for this?",
o:["Add argument-hint so that developers see the expected parameter, such as [table-name], when they invoke the skill",
"Add context: fork so that the forked subagent can ask the developer for the missing table name by itself later on each time",
"Add a required: true field so that Claude Code refuses to run the skill whenever its arguments are missing over time",
"Document the parameter in the project CLAUDE.md so that Claude remembers to ask for it every time the skill is used"],
a:[0],e:"argument-hint frontmatter tells developers what parameter to supply (for example [table-name]) when invoking the skill. context: fork isolates execution, “required: true” is not a skill frontmatter option, and CLAUDE.md is always-loaded context rather than a parameter prompt."},

{s:2,d:3,q:"You are writing a /scaffold skill that should only create and write new files. It must never run shell commands or do anything destructive, even if the developer's request nudges it that way. How do you configure it?",
o:["Set allowed-tools in the skill's frontmatter to just the file-writing tools it needs, so shell access is never granted",
"Write “never run shell commands” at the top of the SKILL.md body so that Claude avoids Bash for the entire run",
"Set context: fork in the frontmatter, since forked skills are sandboxed and cannot run shell commands by default",
"Place the skill in ~/.claude/skills/ because personal skills are limited to file operations and cannot use Bash at all overall"],
a:[0],e:"allowed-tools in skill frontmatter restricts which tools are available during skill execution, which is configuration rather than a request in a prompt. context: fork isolates context but is not a sandbox, and skill location affects sharing rather than permissions."},

{s:2,d:3,q:"A developer wants /review to run extra performance checks in their own sessions, without changing the team's shared /review skill that everyone else relies on. What should they do?",
o:["Create a personal variant under ~/.claude/skills/ with a different name and leave the team's project skill untouched",
"Edit the project skill in .claude/skills/ directly but never commit the change, so that teammates do not receive it",
"Create a personal skill in ~/.claude/skills/ with the same name so that it silently overrides the team's version each time",
"Add a conditional block to the shared SKILL.md that checks the developer's username and changes its behavior for them"],
a:[0],e:"Personal customization belongs in ~/.claude/skills/ under a different name, so it does not affect teammates or create confusing name collisions. Uncommitted edits to shared files are fragile, and per-user conditionals pollute the shared skill."},

{s:2,d:3,q:"You have two pieces of guidance for Claude Code: “all TypeScript must use strict mode,” which applies to every task, and a 12-step release-notes workflow your team runs about twice a month. Where should each live?",
o:["Put strict mode in CLAUDE.md since it always applies, and make release-notes generation an on-demand skill as well",
"Put both in CLAUDE.md so that they are always loaded and Claude never misses either one in the repository",
"Make both into skills so the context window stays small and developers invoke each when they think it is needed",
"Put the release-notes workflow in CLAUDE.md, and make strict mode a skill that developers invoke before each commit"],
a:[0],e:"CLAUDE.md is for always-loaded universal standards, and skills are for on-demand task-specific workflows. Loading a rarely used workflow every session wastes context, and making a universal standard opt-in means it will be missed."},

{s:2,d:3,q:"On a Monday morning you have four tickets queued for Claude Code. Which one should you run with direct execution instead of plan mode?",
o:["A null-pointer crash in one function where the stack trace names the exact line and the right fix is obvious",
"A logging-library swap across 45+ files where several adapter patterns could be applied to the existing calls",
"A choice between queue-based and webhook-based order integrations with very different infrastructure demands as well",
"A restructuring of the auth module so that three services with different session models can share it"],
a:[0],e:"Direct execution fits simple, well-scoped changes with a clear fix, like a single-file bug with a clear stack trace. Large migrations, choices between approaches with infrastructure implications, and architectural restructuring are plan-mode tasks."},

{s:2,d:3,q:"Your team must replace the HTTP client library across about 60 files in a service that handles payments. Which workflow is most effective?",
o:["Use plan mode to explore usages and design the migration, then switch to direct execution to implement the agreed plan",
"Use plan mode for the entire migration including the edits, since plan mode is also the safest way to change files",
"Use direct execution throughout and rely on git to roll back any files that end up migrated in the wrong way",
"Use direct execution for discovery, then switch to plan mode once edits begin so that every edit gets reviewed"],
a:[0],e:"The recommended combination is plan mode for investigation and design, then direct execution to implement the agreed approach. Plan mode is for exploring and designing, not making edits, and skipping planning on a 60-file migration risks costly rework."},

{s:2,d:3,q:"You are mid-refactor of a legacy billing module. The discovery phase (grepping usages, reading dozens of files) has consumed most of the context window before any code is written. What should you do on the next refactor?",
o:["Delegate discovery to the Explore subagent so verbose search output stays isolated and only a summary returns",
"Run discovery in the main session but ask Claude to keep its own replies short to save space in the context each time",
"Disable the Grep and Glob tools so Claude must reason about the codebase structure without searching it at all",
"Start a fresh session for every file under investigation and copy the findings between sessions by hand overall"],
a:[0],e:"The Explore subagent isolates verbose discovery output and returns summaries, preserving the main conversation's context for later phases. Shorter replies do not shrink tool output, disabling search cripples discovery, and manual copying is error-prone."},

{s:2,d:3,q:"You asked Claude Code to convert legacy CSV customer records into your new JSON format by describing the mapping in prose. Each run interprets the description differently, and your last fix broke a case that used to work. What is the most effective next step?",
o:["Give two or three concrete input and output examples that show exactly how sample records should be transformed",
"Rewrite the prose description to be twice as long, with more precise adjectives for the transformation behavior",
"Ask Claude to run the transformation several times and pick whichever output looks the most correct to you",
"Switch to plan mode so Claude writes a detailed plan of the transformation before any code is generated"],
a:[0],e:"Concrete input/output examples are the most effective way to communicate transformations when prose is interpreted inconsistently. More prose keeps the ambiguity, cherry-picking is not repeatable, and plan mode does not resolve an unclear spec."},

{s:2,d:3,q:"You have been asked to add a caching layer to the product-catalog service, but you have never designed one and worry about missing considerations like invalidation and failure behavior. Which technique fits best?",
o:["Ask Claude to interview you about invalidation, failure modes, and constraints before it implements anything at all",
"Ask Claude to implement the most common caching approach first, then review the finished code for problems over time",
"Write a complete specification yourself first and only share it with Claude, without any back-and-forth questions",
"Ask Claude for five full implementations in parallel and pick the one whose tests look the most promising"],
a:[0],e:"The interview pattern has Claude ask questions that surface considerations you may not have anticipated (cache invalidation, failure modes) before implementation. The other options either skip design discovery or depend on knowledge you do not yet have."},

// ---------------- Scenario 3: Multi-Agent Research System ----------------
{s:3,d:1,q:"You run the research platform for an investment firm. The coordinator gathers web results and document analyses, then calls the synthesis subagent with the prompt “Synthesize the findings into a report.” The resulting reports are generic and miss most of the findings the other agents produced. What is the root cause?",
o:["Subagents do not inherit the coordinator's history, so the complete findings must be included in the synthesis prompt",
"The synthesis subagent's context window is too small, so it should be switched to a model with a larger window over time",
"The search subagent did not save its results to shared memory, which every subagent reads automatically when started each time",
"The coordinator's system prompt is too long, so the synthesis subagent truncates the inherited conversation history"],
a:[0],e:"Subagents operate with isolated context. They do not automatically inherit the coordinator's history or share memory. Everything the synthesis agent needs (search results, analysis outputs) must be passed explicitly in its prompt."},

{s:3,d:1,q:"After a refactor, your research coordinator never delegates. Logs show zero Task tool calls, and the coordinator does all of the research itself with the tools it has. The AgentDefinition for each subagent is configured correctly. What is the most likely cause?",
o:["The coordinator's allowedTools does not include “Task”, so it cannot spawn subagents and falls back to doing the work overall",
"The subagent descriptions are too long, so the coordinator decides that the overhead of delegation is not worth it",
"Subagents can only be started from the CLI and not from the SDK, so the coordinator must invoke them with a shell call",
"The coordinator's max_tokens is set too low, which prevents it from writing the larger responses that delegation needs overall"],
a:[0],e:"The Task tool is the mechanism for spawning subagents, and the coordinator's allowedTools must include “Task.” Without it the coordinator has no way to delegate. Subagents are fully supported in the SDK."},

{s:3,d:1,q:"Research requests for your analyst team take far too long. Logs show the coordinator invokes the web-search subagent, waits, invokes the document-analysis subagent, waits, and so on, even though those subtasks do not depend on each other. Which change reduces latency the most?",
o:["Have the coordinator emit multiple Task tool calls in a single response so the independent subagents run in parallel",
"Have each subagent spawn the next one when it finishes, creating a chain that removes the coordinator from the loop overall",
"Merge all subagents into one general-purpose agent with every tool, so that only a single agent run is ever required",
"Raise the coordinator's temperature so that it is more willing to try delegating several tasks during a single turn"],
a:[0],e:"Parallel subagents are spawned by emitting multiple Task tool calls in a single coordinator response instead of across separate turns. Chaining is still sequential and loses coordinator control, and a single mega-agent loses specialization."},

{s:3,d:1,q:"Your search subagent passes findings to the synthesis agent as one long prose summary. The final reports contain accurate claims, but citations are missing or attached to the wrong sources. How should the findings be passed between the agents?",
o:["As structured records that keep each claim separate from its source URL, document name, and page number as well",
"As one long prose summary, asking the synthesis agent to infer the sources from the writing style it sees overall",
"As a list of source URLs only, with the synthesis agent re-fetching every document to build its citations over time",
"As markdown with footnotes, trusting the synthesis agent to keep the footnote numbering consistent throughout"],
a:[0],e:"Structured formats that separate content from metadata (source URL, document name, page number) preserve attribution between agents. Prose compression loses claim-to-source mappings, re-fetching duplicates work, and footnote numbering is fragile."},

{s:3,d:1,q:"Your coordinator sends each subagent a rigid 20-step procedure. When a subagent hits an unexpected source type (a dataset instead of an article, say), it fails or returns poor results. What is the best improvement?",
o:["Rewrite the prompts around research goals and quality criteria instead of rigid step-by-step procedures",
"Add even more procedural steps covering the unexpected source types so that every possible case is spelled out",
"Remove the prompts entirely and rely on each subagent's description field to tell it how to do the research",
"Replace the subagents with fixed scripts, since a procedural workflow is better expressed as code than as a prompt"],
a:[0],e:"Coordinator prompts should specify goals and quality criteria rather than step-by-step procedures, so subagents can adapt to what they encounter. Adding steps cannot anticipate every case, and descriptions are for selection rather than task instructions."},

{s:3,d:1,q:"Simple factual queries from your team (“When was the EU AI Act passed?”) run through all four subagents and take three minutes, while only complex queries need the full pipeline. What is the best design change?",
o:["Have the coordinator assess each query and invoke only the subagents it needs, instead of the full pipeline every time",
"Remove the document-analysis and report agents completely, since most queries do not appear to require them at all each time",
"Run the full pipeline anyway but with a cheaper model for each subagent, to bring the cost of simple queries down over time",
"Add a cache of previous reports and return the closest match whenever a simple query arrives at the coordinator as well"],
a:[0],e:"Coordinators should analyze query requirements and dynamically select which subagents to invoke based on complexity, rather than always routing through the full pipeline. Removing agents breaks complex queries, and cheaper models do not fix latency."},

{s:3,d:1,q:"Reports from your research system are coherent but often thin on important subtopics. You want the system itself to detect and fill these gaps before a report reaches the analyst. What pattern should you implement?",
o:["Have the coordinator check the synthesis for gaps, re-delegate targeted searches, and re-run synthesis until it is complete",
"Instruct the synthesis agent to fill any gaps using its own background knowledge so that every report looks complete",
"Run the entire pipeline three times from scratch and merge all three reports together into one final combined report",
"Ask the report agent to add a disclaimer listing the topics that it suspects might be missing from the final report"],
a:[0],e:"This is an iterative refinement loop: the coordinator evaluates synthesis output for gaps, re-delegates targeted queries to search and analysis, and re-invokes synthesis until coverage is sufficient. Filling gaps from background knowledge loses provenance, and full reruns are wasteful."},

{s:3,d:1,q:"You run two web-search subagents in parallel on the same topic, a market-sizing report. About 70% of the sources they return overlap, wasting time and tokens. What is the best fix?",
o:["Assign each search subagent a distinct subtopic or source type so that their research scopes no longer overlap overall",
"Let both subagents search freely and have the synthesis agent remove duplicate sources when it combines findings",
"Reduce to a single search subagent with a higher result limit so duplicate sources can never appear between agents",
"Have the subagents share a memory store and check it before each search so they skip sources the other already found"],
a:[0],e:"Partition research scope across subagents (distinct subtopics or source types) to minimize duplication. Deduplicating later still wastes the work, one agent loses parallelism, and subagents do not share memory by default, so coordination belongs with the coordinator."},

{s:3,d:1,q:"To cut latency, a teammate proposes letting your research subagents message each other directly instead of routing everything through the coordinator. What is the strongest argument for keeping the current design?",
o:["Routing all communication through the coordinator gives observability, consistent error handling, and control over information flow",
"Direct subagent-to-subagent messaging is better, since removing the coordinator from the loop always improves reliability",
"Letting subagents talk directly is fine as long as every message is logged, which gives the same guarantees as routing going forward",
"Replacing the coordinator with a shared message bus that every subagent can publish to and read from is the best option in this case"],
a:[0],e:"Hub-and-spoke: the coordinator manages all inter-subagent communication, error handling, and information routing. That gives observability, consistent error handling, and controlled information flow. Logging alone does not provide centralized control or recovery."},

{s:3,d:5,q:"Two credible sources used by your market report disagree: one says the sector grew 23%, the other says 31%, both over similar time periods. How should the synthesis step handle this?",
o:["Include both values with their source attribution and annotate the conflict instead of choosing one arbitrarily over time",
"Report the average of the two values, since averaging credible sources tends to produce the best overall estimate",
"Report the value from the more recently retrieved source and drop the other, since newer retrievals are more reliable",
"Leave the statistic out of the report entirely, since conflicting values show that neither source can be trusted"],
a:[0],e:"Conflicting statistics from credible sources should be preserved with attribution and explicitly annotated, not resolved by arbitrary selection, averaging, or omission. Retrieval time says nothing about reliability."},

{s:3,d:5,q:"The synthesis agent tells your analyst that two sources “contradict each other” on smartphone adoption rates. On inspection, one source is from 2019 and the other is from 2024. How do you prevent this misreading in the future?",
o:["Require subagents to include publication or data-collection dates in their structured outputs so differences are read correctly",
"Instruct the synthesis agent to always prefer whichever statistic is larger, since larger figures reflect recent growth",
"Filter out every source older than one year so that the system only compares data from the same time period",
"Flag every numerical difference as a contradiction and send each of them to a human reviewer before the report ships"],
a:[0],e:"Requiring publication or collection dates in structured outputs prevents temporal differences from being misread as contradictions. Preferring larger numbers is arbitrary, blanket age filters discard valuable trend data, and flagging everything overloads reviewers."},

{s:3,d:5,q:"The document-analysis subagent is assigned 10 filings. It analyzes 8 successfully, but 2 return access-denied errors from the document store. What should it return to the coordinator?",
o:["The eight successful analyses plus structured error details for the two failures, including what was attempted over time",
"A failure for the entire task, because an incomplete analysis could mislead the synthesis agent further downstream as well",
"The eight successful analyses presented as the complete set, since the two failed filings are probably not important",
"Nothing yet, retrying the two failed filings indefinitely until access is granted by the document store administrators over time"],
a:[0],e:"Return partial results along with structured error context (failure type, what was attempted) so the coordinator can decide how to recover. Failing everything wastes good work, silently dropping failures hides gaps, and access-denied is not transient, so endless retry is wrong."},

{s:3,d:5,q:"Your web-search subagent occasionally gets a single HTTP 503 from the search provider, which usually succeeds on a quick retry. How should this failure be handled?",
o:["Retry the transient failure locally inside the subagent, and propagate it to the coordinator only if it cannot be resolved",
"Immediately propagate every failure to the coordinator, since the coordinator must make every single retry decision",
"Silently skip the failed search and keep going, since one failed query is unlikely to affect the final report much in practice",
"Terminate the subagent and have the coordinator restart the whole research pipeline from the very first step again"],
a:[0],e:"Subagents should implement local recovery for transient failures and propagate only the errors they cannot resolve, including what was attempted and any partial results. Silent skipping hides gaps, and restarting everything is disproportionate."},

{s:3,d:5,q:"During a research run for a client brief, a key database of academic papers was unavailable for the entire session. The rest of the research succeeded. How should the final synthesis reflect this?",
o:["Annotate the synthesis to show which findings are well supported and which topic areas have gaps from unavailable sources",
"Present all findings with equal confidence so that readers are not distracted by technical details of source availability overall",
"Delay the report until every source is available again, no matter how long the outage takes to resolve on its own as a result",
"Fill the gaps with the synthesis agent's general knowledge, labeled as research so that the report appears complete"],
a:[0],e:"Synthesis output should include coverage annotations that show which findings are well supported and which topic areas have gaps due to unavailable sources. Hiding gaps misleads readers, and filling them with unsourced knowledge breaks provenance."},

{s:3,d:2,q:"Every subagent in your research system has access to all 18 tools. The synthesis agent keeps launching web searches in the middle of synthesis instead of working with the findings it was handed. What is the best fix?",
o:["Restrict each subagent to the tools its role needs, so the synthesis agent no longer has any web-search tools",
"Keep all 18 tools on every agent but add a system prompt rule telling the synthesis agent to avoid web search",
"Give the synthesis agent even more tools so it has better alternatives available whenever it is tempted to search",
"Rename the web-search tools with an internal_ prefix so that the synthesis agent is less inclined to select them"],
a:[0],e:"Too many tools (18 versus 4 to 5) degrade selection reliability, and agents with tools outside their specialization tend to misuse them. Scope each subagent's tools to its role. Prompt rules are probabilistic, and renaming is a hack."},

// ---------------- Scenario 6: Structured Data Extraction ----------------
{s:6,d:4,q:"You maintain an accounts-payable pipeline that asks Claude to return extracted invoice data as JSON in its text response. About 2% of responses have syntax errors (trailing commas, unescaped quotes) that break the downstream parser. What is the most reliable fix?",
o:["Define an extraction tool whose input schema is the target JSON schema, and read the data from the tool_use block",
"Ask the model to respond with JSON only, and wrap the parser in a try/except that retries whenever parsing fails",
"Add three examples of correctly formatted JSON to the prompt so that the model copies the exact output structure overall",
"Post-process the text output with regular expressions that repair common JSON syntax errors before it is parsed as well"],
a:[0],e:"Tool use with a JSON schema is the most reliable way to get schema-compliant structured output, and it eliminates JSON syntax errors. Retries and regex repair treat symptoms, and examples reduce but do not eliminate syntax failures."},

{s:6,d:4,q:"Your intake pipeline has three extraction tools (invoice, receipt, contract), and the document type is unknown in advance. With tool_choice set to “auto,” the model sometimes replies with prose instead of calling any tool. What should you change?",
o:["Set tool_choice to “any” so the model must call one of the extraction tools but can pick the schema that fits each time",
"Keep tool_choice at “auto” and add a sentence asking the model to please always call one of the extraction tools as well",
"Force the invoice extraction tool on every request, since invoices are the most common document type in the system over time",
"Remove all but one extraction tool and merge every field into one large schema that covers all of the document types"],
a:[0],e:"tool_choice “any” guarantees the model calls a tool (no plain text) while letting it choose which one, which fits multiple schemas with an unknown document type. “auto” allows text, and forcing one tool breaks the other document types."},

{s:6,d:4,q:"In your extraction flow, extract_metadata must always run before any enrichment tools (vendor lookup, currency conversion). How should you guarantee this ordering?",
o:["Set tool_choice to {“type”: “tool”, “name”: “extract_metadata”} on the first call, then handle enrichment afterward",
"Set tool_choice to “any” and list extract_metadata first, since the model calls tools in the order they are defined overall",
"Set tool_choice to “auto” and explain in the system prompt that extract_metadata must always be the first tool called",
"Give extract_metadata a name that starts with 01_ so the model sorts it first and selects it ahead of the others each time"],
a:[0],e:"Forced tool selection ({“type”: “tool”, “name”: …}) guarantees that a specific tool is called. Run it first, then process enrichment in follow-up turns. Tool list order and naming do not control selection, and prompt instructions are probabilistic."},

{s:6,d:4,q:"Your schema marks purchase_order_number as required. Many invoices from small vendors have no PO number, and you discover the model invents plausible-looking ones for them. What is the best fix?",
o:["Make the field optional and nullable so the model can return null when the document simply does not contain it",
"Keep the field required and add a prompt instruction telling the model to never invent a purchase order number over time",
"Keep the field required and validate it against a regex so that fabricated numbers with a bad format get rejected",
"Remove the field from the schema entirely and ask downstream teams to look up purchase order numbers by hand each time"],
a:[0],e:"Required fields pressure the model to fabricate values to satisfy the schema. Making fields optional or nullable when source documents may lack the information prevents this. A regex cannot catch well-formatted fabrications, and removing the field loses real data."},

{s:6,d:4,q:"Your document_category enum has 8 values. New document types keep appearing, and the model forces them into the closest wrong category. Some documents are genuinely ambiguous. What schema change is best?",
o:["Add an “other” value to the enum with a companion detail string, plus “unclear” for genuinely ambiguous documents",
"Replace the enum with a free-text string field so the model can describe the category in whatever words it likes overall",
"Keep the enum as it is, since forcing every document into a known category keeps downstream reporting consistent overall",
"Add every conceivable category to the enum up front so the model never meets a document with a missing category each time"],
a:[0],e:"The recommended pattern is an enum plus “other” with a detail string for extensibility, and “unclear” for ambiguous cases. Free text loses consistency, forcing wrong categories corrupts data, and you cannot enumerate every future category."},

{s:6,d:4,q:"You use tool_use with a strict JSON schema for invoices. Outputs always parse and validate, but sometimes the line items do not add up to the stated total. Why does this happen, and what helps?",
o:["Schemas prevent syntax errors, not semantic ones, so add validation that compares the summed line items with the total",
"The schema is not strict enough, so adding more required fields will force the model to compute totals correctly",
"Tool use is unreliable for numbers, so switch to returning JSON in a text block and parse it with a JSON library",
"The model is too small for arithmetic, so switch to the largest model and the line-item mismatches will stop entirely"],
a:[0],e:"Strict schemas via tool use eliminate syntax errors but not semantic errors (values that do not sum, values in the wrong field). Add semantic validation (for example with Pydantic) and a retry-with-feedback loop. More required fields or a bigger model do not close the gap."},

{s:6,d:4,q:"Validation fails on an invoice because invoice_date was extracted as “March 4, 2024” but your schema requires YYYY-MM-DD. What should the retry request include?",
o:["The original document, the failed extraction, and the specific validation error, so the model can correct its own output",
"Only the specific validation error message, since resending the whole document wastes tokens on each retry attempt",
"The original document alone with the same prompt, since a fresh attempt is usually enough to fix formatting problems",
"A generic note saying the output was invalid, so the model re-examines everything instead of only the flagged field"],
a:[0],e:"Retry with error feedback: send the original document, the failed extraction, and the specific validation errors so the model can self-correct. Without the document it cannot re-check, and without the specific error it may repeat the mistake."},

{s:6,d:4,q:"Validation fails because contract_end_date is required but came back empty. You find the end date is stated only in a separate amendment document that was never included in the request. Will a retry with error feedback fix this?",
o:["No, because the information is absent from the source; make the field nullable or supply the amendment document each time",
"Yes, because retrying with the validation error will lead the model to find the date if it looks more carefully",
"Yes, because retrying at a higher temperature will make the model explore more of the document to locate the date",
"No, because the model cannot read dates, so a separate regex-based parser must extract the date field in future"],
a:[0],e:"Retries work for format and structural errors but are ineffective when the information is simply absent from the provided source. Fix the input (provide the amendment) or the schema (nullable). Pushing harder invites fabrication."},

{s:6,d:4,q:"Extraction works well on invoices with line-item tables, but on invoices that describe charges in narrative paragraphs, required fields often come back null. What is the most effective fix?",
o:["Add few-shot examples showing correct extraction from varied formats, such as narrative text versus line-item tables",
"Make every field required so that the model is forced to search harder for the values instead of returning nulls overall",
"Add an instruction to look carefully everywhere in the document, stressing that missing values are unacceptable",
"Preprocess all of the documents into one standard template by hand before sending them to the model to extract as well"],
a:[0],e:"Few-shot examples that demonstrate correct extraction from varied document structures address empty or null extraction for fields that are present in an unfamiliar format. Forcing required fields invites fabrication, and manual templating does not scale."},

{s:6,d:4,q:"Some vendor invoices are internally inconsistent: the printed total does not match the sum of the printed line items. Your finance team needs to know when this happens. What schema design is best?",
o:["Extract both stated_total and calculated_total with a conflict_detected boolean so discrepancies are flagged downstream",
"Extract only stated_total, since the total printed on the document is the authoritative value for downstream systems over time",
"Extract only calculated_total and quietly correct the document, so downstream systems always receive a correct sum each time",
"Reject any invoice whose totals disagree and ask the vendor to resend it before any extraction is attempted on it"],
a:[0],e:"Self-correction validation flows extract calculated_total alongside stated_total and add a conflict_detected boolean for inconsistent source data. That surfaces the discrepancy instead of hiding it in either direction."},

{s:6,d:4,q:"You submitted 10,000 archived contracts through the Message Batches API overnight. In the morning, 140 failed with context-length-exceeded errors. What is the best next step?",
o:["Resubmit only the 140 failed documents, identified by custom_id, after chunking them to fit within the context limit",
"Resubmit the entire batch of 10,000 documents, since partial batches make the results hard to correlate afterward",
"Resubmit the 140 failed documents unchanged, because context-length errors in the batch API are usually transient",
"Move all future processing to the synchronous API, since the batch API cannot handle documents of varying lengths"],
a:[0],e:"Handle batch failures by resubmitting only the failed items (identified by custom_id) with appropriate changes, here chunking oversized documents. Context-length errors are not transient, and custom_id makes correlating partial resubmissions straightforward."},

{s:6,d:4,q:"Documents arrive continuously and your SLA says each must be fully processed within 30 hours of arrival. The batch API can take up to 24 hours, and your post-processing takes 2 hours. What is the longest interval you can wait between batch submissions?",
o:["Every 4 hours, since a document can wait one full interval, then 24 hours of batch time plus 2 hours of post-processing",
"Every 6 hours, since 30 hours minus the 24-hour batch window leaves six hours to wait for the next submission for the team",
"Once per day, since the batch window is 24 hours and submitting more often than that gives no benefit at all in practice",
"Every 30 hours, matching the SLA, so that each batch holds exactly one SLA period's worth of incoming documents over time"],
a:[0],e:"Worst case: a document arrives right after a submission and waits a full interval, then up to 24 hours of batch processing, then 2 hours of post-processing. 30 − 24 − 2 = 4 hours. The 6-hour answer forgets post-processing."},

{s:6,d:4,q:"Your extraction flow needs the model to call a lookup_vendor tool mid-extraction, receive the result, and then finish the extraction. A teammate wants to move this flow to the Message Batches API for the 50% savings. What is the key issue?",
o:["Batch requests cannot execute tools mid-request and return results, so this multi-turn lookup flow needs another design",
"Batch is ideal here, since each request can call the vendor database tool as often as it needs within 24 hours",
"Batch works only if the vendor lookup tool is declared with tool_choice “any” so the model must call it each time",
"Batch is unsuitable only because results come back out of order, which makes the vendor lookups hard to match up"],
a:[0],e:"The batch API does not support multi-turn tool calling within a single request. You cannot execute a tool mid-request and return its result. Out-of-order results are handled by custom_id, so that is not the blocker."},

{s:6,d:4,q:"You are about to run a new extraction prompt on 200,000 archived documents through the batch API. What is the most cost-effective approach?",
o:["Refine the prompt on a representative sample first, then submit the full volume to maximize first-pass success overall",
"Submit the full volume immediately, then refine the prompt from the failures and resubmit until the error rate is low",
"Submit the full volume twice with two different prompts and keep whichever result is better for each document",
"Skip prompt testing, since the 50% batch discount makes resubmitting failed documents cheaper than testing up front each time"],
a:[0],e:"Refining the prompt on a sample set before batch-processing large volumes maximizes first-pass success and reduces iterative resubmission costs. Even at 50% off, re-running large volumes is expensive."},

{s:6,d:5,q:"Your extraction system reports 97% overall accuracy, and leadership wants to cut human review to save headcount. Which TWO steps should come first? (Select TWO.)",
o:["Analyze accuracy by document type and field to confirm that performance is consistent across all of the segments",
"Run stratified random sampling of high-confidence extractions to measure error rates and spot novel patterns",
"Remove human review for all documents, since 97% accuracy exceeds what most human reviewers achieve in practice",
"Route only documents with self-reported confidence under 99% to humans, without validating that confidence first",
"Reduce review to a random 3% of documents on the assumption that errors are spread evenly across all doc types"],
a:[0,1],e:"Aggregate metrics can mask poor performance on specific document types or fields, so validate by segment first. Stratified random sampling of high-confidence extractions measures ongoing error rates and catches novel patterns. Uncalibrated confidence and evenly-distributed-error assumptions are unsafe."}
  ]
});
